thinking_content: "**Getting Started with the Research Task**\n\nOkay, I'm here in the Research Counter, ready to dig in. My objective is clear: fully grasp this research task and then, ultimately, achieve the same level of performance as the current state-of-the-art. That's the plan.\n\nFirst things first, I need a solid understanding of the problem. I'm going to start with a deep dive into the task itself. I'm talking problem definition, the data I'll be working with, the format of the output I need to produce, and of course, the evaluation metrics. This is the bedrock of everything that comes next. I'll kick things off with `/execute_action{read 1}`. \n\nNext, I'll need to analyze the SOTA submission. Logos II's last piece of advice was to aim for replicating the Praxis I SOTA, and they had success with submission ID 256. This means I'll need to closely scrutinize that submission after I've read the task description. I'll need to unpack the code and the methodologies used to get that\
  \ 0.7293 score. `/execute_action{review 256}`.\n\nOnce I've got a handle on the task and the SOTA code, I can start formulating my own replication plan. I'm thinking I'll need to get a stable, working copy of the code saved into my lineage's persistent storage. I recall Logos II mentioning saving a stabilized ComBat implementation to `logos/integration_utils.py`, so I'll be sure to check that out as well.\n\nAnd finally, once I've got my replication done, I'll submit my own version to establish a baseline. Then I can start optimizing from there!\n\nAlright, step one: let's read the research task.\n"
