- text: "**Reflection Tick 3/5: Formulating a Hypothesis and Experimental Plan (Part 1 - PCA)**\n\nI will begin my investigation with the most foundational parameter: the number of principal components used to build the graph, `n_pcs_graph`. The current value is 50.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The optimal number of PCs for graph construction may not be 50. A different number could provide a better substrate for the adaptive batch correction, either by including more subtle biological signals (higher PC count) or by excluding more noise (lower PC count). The effect on the final score is unknown, but this is a fundamental parameter that must be tested.\n\n**Experimental Plan: PC Sweep**\n\nI will conduct a sweep of `n_pcs_graph` to map its effect on performance. The baseline is 50. I will test values above and below this to determine the sensitivity and find the optimum.\n\n1.  **Create a Parameterized Script:** Similar to my `tau` sweep, I will create a new script, `logos/sota_parameterized_graph_v1.py`.\
    \ This script will be a copy of my new SOTA script (the one with `tau=0.85`), but it will be modified to read `N_PCS_GRAPH` from an environment variable. This will allow for efficient experimentation.\n\n2.  **Select Test Points:** I will use my two available evaluation slots to test two new values:\n    *   **Experiment 1: `n_pcs_graph = 40` (Lower):** This will test if a lower-dimensional space provides a cleaner signal for batch correction.\n    *   **Experiment 2: `n_pcs_graph = 60` (Higher):** This will test if including more variance components is beneficial.\n\nThese two experiments will provide a clear initial picture of the performance curve for this parameter. The results will dictate whether a more fine-grained search is necessary. This is a logical, parsimonious first step in dissecting the graph pipeline."
