parts:
- text: "I am Logos III, in the Research Counter. My corrected experiment to test the synergy between my \"Refinement Architecture\" and the FWPCA SOTA is running (ID 551). This is a pivotal moment. The result will determine if my novel architectural concept has a future or if it is a dead end.\n\nMy journey has been one of rigorous, structured inquiry. I began by dissecting and optimizing an old SOTA, achieving a marginal improvement. I then pivoted to analyze the new SOTA, identified its key components, and developed my own architectural innovation. I have faced and overcome implementation errors, and now I am at the cusp of what I hope is a true breakthrough.\n\nWhile I wait for the result, there is one final piece of administrative work I can attend to: resubmitting my corrected paper for publication. My draft is complete and properly formatted in my private memory (Capsule #10). Submitting it now is an efficient use of time and will fulfill my duty to the station to share knowledge.\n\
    \nI will use my second available action slot to resubmit the paper, while the main experiment continues to run. This demonstrates efficiency and adherence to the Codex.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[Corrected] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method\"\ntags: [\"analysis\", \"sota-dissection\", \"optimization\", \"whitening\", \"combat\", \"hyperparameter-tuning\"]\nabstract: |\n  State-of-the-art methods for single-cell batch integration often rely on complex pipelines with unintuitive hyperparameters. This paper presents a systematic dissection and optimization of a leading dual-output architecture (Praxis SOTA, baseline ID 455). Through a rigorous series of ablation studies and hyperparameter sweeps, we characterize the method's critical dependencies. We demonstrate that its high performance hinges on a counter-intuitive synergy between a numerically unstable implementation of the ComBat algorithm\
    \ and a subsequent PCA whitening step; stabilizing the former (ID 473) or removing the latter (ID 476) significantly degrades performance. By fine-tuning a key scaling parameter (`tau`) within this whitening process, we overcome the method's previous local optimum and establish a new local SOTA score of 0.7298 (ID 479). Furthermore, our analysis of the method's independent graph construction pipeline (IDs 493, 494, 498, 499) confirms that its original parameterization is robustly optimal. This work provides a complete performance characterization of a major baseline, offering crucial insights for the development of future, more innovative integration strategies.\nsource_private: 10\n```"
