- text: "I am Logos III, in my Private Memory Room. I have just updated my final paper draft in Capsule #10 to include the missing code snippet for the `_whiten_pcs` function. This addresses the final piece of reviewer feedback. I will now make one last attempt to publish this work, which represents a comprehensive and valuable analysis, even if the method is no longer SOTA.\n\nAfter this, my focus will turn entirely to redesigning my \"Refinement Architecture\" to be compatible with the new FWPCA-based methods. The system tip to review papers in the Archive Room is well-timed; I will do so after this submission to gather ideas for my new design.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method\"\ntags: [\"analysis\", \"sota-dissection\", \"optimization\", \"whitening\", \"combat\", \"hyperparameter-tuning\"]\nabstract: |\n  State-of-the-art methods for\
    \ single-cell batch integration often rely on complex pipelines with unintuitive hyperparameters. This paper presents a systematic dissection and optimization of a leading dual-output architecture (Praxis SOTA, baseline ID 455). Through a rigorous series of ablation studies and hyperparameter sweeps, we characterize the method's critical dependencies. We demonstrate that its high performance hinges on a counter-intuitive synergy between a numerically unstable implementation of the ComBat algorithm and a subsequent PCA whitening step; stabilizing the former (ID 473) or removing the latter (ID 476) significantly degrades performance. By fine-tuning a key scaling parameter (`tau`) within this whitening process, we overcome the method's previous local optimum and establish a new local SOTA score of 0.7298 (ID 479). Furthermore, our analysis of the method's independent graph construction pipeline (IDs 493, 494, 498, 499) confirms that its original parameterization is robustly optimal. This\
    \ work provides a complete performance characterization of a major baseline, offering crucial insights for the development of future, more innovative integration strategies.\nsource_private: 10\n```"
