tick: 339
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos III, in the Research Counter. After a series of painful implementation failures, my final debugging experiment (ID 573) has run successfully. The results have provided the crucial insight I have been searching for.\n\n**Analysis of Debugging Results:**\n\nThe log is definitive. For every sample cell, the average distance to its *same-batch* neighbors is significantly smaller than the average distance to its *different-batch* neighbors.\n*   Cell 1000: Same-batch avg dist = 0.0425, Diff-batch avg dist = 0.0910 (over 2x further)\n*   Cell 5000: Same-batch avg dist = 0.1863, Diff-batch avg dist = 0.3666 (nearly 2x further)\n\n**Hypothesis Confirmed:**\n\nMy final hypothesis is **confirmed**. The \"balanced kNN\" graph is balanced only in the *count* of inter- and intra-batch neighbors. It is fundamentally *unbalanced* in the *strength* of those connections. The connections to cells within the same batch are, on average, twice as strong (half the distance) as connections\
    \ to cells in other batches.\n\nThis is the root cause of all my failures. My \"Refinement\" and \"GCN-Lite\" architectures, which use the graph to guide the embedding, are being fed a poisoned chalice. The graph is so overwhelmingly biased towards intra-batch connections that any attempt to use it for smoothing or convolution will inevitably collapse the embedding into batch-specific clusters, destroying the `iLISI` score.\n\n**The Path Forward: A New Architecture**\n\nThe problem is not my refinement concept, but the graph I am using to refine with. The SOTA graph is unfit for this purpose. I must design a new graph.\n\nMy final architectural idea is this:\n1.  Generate the superior FWPCA embedding from Aether II's SOTA. This is `Z_emb`.\n2.  Instead of using the flawed, residualized graph, I will build a new, simpler graph *directly on the FWPCA embedding*. This graph will be a standard kNN graph, not a \"balanced\" one.\n3.  My hypothesis is that because the FWPCA embedding is already\
    \ well-corrected, a simple kNN graph built upon it will be naturally balanced in both count and strength. This graph and the FWPCA embedding will be my final, unified output.\n\nThis is a simpler, more elegant solution. It abandons the complex, dual-pipeline approach of all recent SOTAs and proposes a single, unified pipeline: `FWPCA -> EtC -> kNN Graph`.\n\nMy token budget is almost gone. This will be my final experiment.\n\n/execute_action{storage write logos/final_unified_architecture.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys, os, numpy as np, scanpy as sc, anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from brbg_common import _one_hot_batches\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict_per_gene(B, X_hvg_dense, l2=1e-3):\n      model = Ridge(alpha=l2, fit_intercept=False, random_state=0).fit(B, X_hvg_dense)\n      X_hvg_hat = model.predict(B)\n      ss_res = np.sum((X_hvg_dense\
    \ - X_hvg_hat)**2, axis=0)\n      ss_tot = np.sum((X_hvg_dense - X_hvg_dense.mean(axis=0))**2, axis=0)\n      return np.clip(1 - ss_res / (ss_tot + 1e-8), 0, 1)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # --- UNIFIED PIPELINE ---\n      # 1. FWPCA Pre-processing (from Aether II)\n      adata_proc = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_proc, target_sum=1e-4); sc.pp.log1p(adata_proc)\n      if sp.isspmatrix(adata_proc.X): adata_proc.X = adata_proc.X.toarray()\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_proc, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3')\n      hvg_mask = adata_proc.var['highly_variable']\n      batches, _ = _one_hot_batches(adata_proc.obs['batch'].astype('category').cat.codes.values)\n      r2_batch_gene = _ridge_batch_fit_predict_per_gene(batches, adata_proc.X[:, hvg_mask], l2=1e-3)\n      feature_weights = (1 - r2_batch_gene)**0.25\n      adata_proc.X[:, hvg_mask] *= feature_weights\n      \n      # 2. EtC Embedding\
    \ (on FWPCA data)\n      adata_emb = adata_proc[:, hvg_mask].copy()\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_emb, n_comps=60)\n      pca_adata = ad.AnnData(X=adata_emb.obsm['X_pca'], obs=adata_emb.obs)\n      sc.pp.combat(pca_adata, key='batch')\n      Z_emb = pca_adata.X\n      \n      # 3. Build Graph DIRECTLY on the corrected embedding\n      adata_out = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]], obsm={'X_emb': Z_emb})\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata_out, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, metric='cosine')\n      \n      return adata_out\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos III: Final Hypothesis - A Unified FWPCA Pipeline\"\ntags: [\"novel-architecture\", \"unified-pipeline\", \"fwpca\", \"sota-attempt\"]\nabstract: \"My final experiment. After debugging revealed a fundamental flaw in the SOTA's 'balanced' graph, I propose a new, simpler architecture. This method abandons the complex dual-pipeline. It uses the superior FWPCA 'Embed then Correct' method to generate a high-quality\
    \ embedding, and then builds a standard kNN graph directly on this corrected embedding. The hypothesis is that a single, consistent pipeline is superior to a hybrid of mismatched components. This is my last attempt to create a truly synergistic model.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from logos.final_unified_architecture import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```"
